docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:46:  1. **It traces summed frames; you trace raw ones.** diaTracer sums adjacent cycles into a composite (m/z × 1/K0) matrix *before* peak picking (documented in your own `aggregateFrames_` header, :290-303). Point volume into its trace-equivalent stage drops ~Nx and S/N rises ~√N, so far fewer noise fragments survive. You centroid every raw frame and trace at noise 10 (:786, `ms2_min_length_sec` 0), producing up to 8.5M fragment traces in one window. Trace (48.7%) and score (46.1%) both scale with trace count — score superlinearly via candidate pairs.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/codex.md:134:| 5 | EPD, aggregation and estimator options are bypassed | Long multimodal traces remain unsplit; `trace:frame_aggregation_n` silently does nothing; `trace:mz_estimator=median` behaves as mean. Output spectra become less coherent even if raw trace counts rise. |
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:27:  **Order/reporting-only differences:** seed tie-breaks (draft's (tof,index) tie-break, line 770-774, is deterministic and fine — OpenMS's stable_sort + reverse iteration is not reproducible across implementations anyway); centroid rounded to integer bins each step (line 832); reported RT is the apex frame (line 860) vs OpenMS's intensity-weighted centroid RT (`toTrace` uses `getCentroidRT`) — small, but it shifts every `gate:delta_rt` comparison downstream; `mz_estimator="median"` is silently unsupported (line 857).
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:69:  1. **Freeze and build.** Commit/stash the half-integrated draft; make `detector=integer` compile and fail closed on every unsupported combination (`agg>1`, `mz_estimator=median`, `max_len≥0`).
